imple statement with she takes negative **doesn’t she**.","C":"This has the same polarity, rather than the ordinary checking tag requested.","D":"The lexical present-simple verb requires does in the tag, not is."}}},{"id":"8f1b3cc6-9bde-56d1-a2e5-a16068f9dda9","topicSlug":"question-tags","level":"B1","prompt":"“They did not repair the rowing boat yesterday, ____?”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"did they"},{"id":"B","text":"didn’t they"},{"id":"C","text":"do they"},{"id":"D","text":"were they"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"A negative clause takes a **positive checking tag**.","explanation":"The negative past-simple clause takes the positive tag **did they**.","keywords":["negative-past-tag"],"skill":"negative-past-tag","contextKey":"question-tags-50","fingerprint":"be9dd05fe7349d01e0ea231a23c93b1d2a2b06d45723f898c5580e80f77ee670","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-question-tags"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["A negative clause takes a **positive checking tag**.","The negative past-simple clause takes the positive tag **did they**.","Choose **did they**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The negative past-simple clause takes the positive tag **did they**.","B":"The main clause is already negative; the ordinary checking tag is positive.","C":"Do changes the tense to present.","D":"Were does not match the did auxiliary."}}},{"id":"cb73f87b-5160-5360-a008-5f19d1281d0f","topicSlug":"question-tags","level":"B1","prompt":"“We are repairing the rowing boat, ____?”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"aren’t we"},{"id":"B","text":"don’t we"},{"id":"C","text":"isn’t we"},{"id":"D","text":"weren’t we"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"The statement already contains **are**; keep that auxiliary family.","explanation":"Reuse **are** and the pronoun we, with negative polarity.","keywords":["continuous-tag"],"skill":"continuous-tag","contextKey":"question-tags-50","fingerprint":"64e049448e7dbb3aab71ca493455e799e02895fbc30e99d57c17300a86536062","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-question-tags"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The statement already contains **are**; keep that auxiliary family.","Reuse **are** and the pronoun we, with negative polarity.","Choose **aren’t we**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Reuse **are** and the pronoun we, with negative polarity.","B":"The existing auxiliary is are, not do.","C":"Is does not agree with we.","D":"Were changes the time to past."}}},{"id":"b969b8af-54fb-5f20-aafe-3f60b55718e6","topicSlug":"question-tags","level":"B1","prompt":"“Let’s repair the rowing boat, ____?”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"aren’t we"},{"id":"B","text":"did we"},{"id":"C","text":"shall we"},{"id":"D","text":"do we"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Recognise the special **let’s … shall we?** pattern.","explanation":"The usual tag after a shared suggestion with **let’s** is **shall we**.","keywords":["suggestion-tag"],"skill":"suggestion-tag","contextKey":"question-tags-50","fingerprint":"973554b0814ee512fcf17347630678c23f79d17be174fba87e58afa2299a2f98","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-question-tags"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Recognise the special **let’s … shall we?** pattern.","The usual tag after a shared suggestion with **let’s** is **shall we**.","Choose **shall we**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The clause has no be construction to match.","B":"A past tag does not fit this present suggestion.","C":"The usual tag after a shared suggestion with **let’s** is **shall we**.","D":"Do we is not the usual let’s suggestion tag."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Add a short checking question and match its auxiliary, pronoun and polarity to the statement.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Opposite polarity in a checking tag","explanation":"A usual checking tag is negative after a positive statement and positive after a negative statement. Intonation helps show whether the speaker expects agreement or is genuinely checking. Other tag patterns exist in conversation, but the ordinary opposite-polarity form is a useful starting point.","pattern":"She is ready, **isn’t she**? / She isn’t ready, **is she**?","commonMistakes":["A negative statement can contain never or nobody without the word not. Its checking tag is normally positive."],"examples":["“She prepares the visitor guide every week, **doesn’t she**?”","“She restores the old portrait every week, **doesn’t she**?”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Reuse the auxiliary","explanation":"Match the tense and auxiliary of the statement. If it has no auxiliary and is ordinary present or past simple, use do, does or did. Replace a noun subject with an appropriate pronoun.","pattern":"The guide arrived, **didn’t she**?","commonMistakes":["Do not use is as a universal tag auxiliary; it must match the clause."],"examples":["“They did not prepare the visitor guide yesterday, **did they**?”","“They did not restore the old portrait yesterday, **did they**?”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Keep number and person","explanation":"The tag’s pronoun refers to the statement’s subject, not the nearest noun. Everyone commonly takes they in a tag. There stays there after an existential statement. Standard conversation often uses aren’t I after I am.","pattern":"There is a seat, **isn’t there**? / I’m late, **aren’t I**?","commonMistakes":["Everyone is ready, aren’t they? combines singular agreement in the main clause with singular they in the tag."],"examples":["“We are preparing the visitor guide, **aren’t we**?”","“We are restoring the old portrait, **aren’t we**?”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Requests and special tags","explanation":"Commands often use will you or would you as a softening tag. Let’s commonly takes shall we. These patterns do not follow the simple positive/negative statement rule. Tags in these exercises are ordinary checking tags unless a special function is named.","pattern":"Let’s leave, **shall we**? / Close the door, **will you**?","commonMistakes":["A comma normally separates a written tag from the statement."],"examples":["“Let’s prepare the visitor guide, **shall we**?”","“Let’s restore the old portrait, **shall we**?”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["She is ready, **isn’t she**? / She isn’t ready, **is she**?","The guide arrived, **didn’t she**?","There is a seat, **isn’t there**? / I’m late, **aren’t I**?","Let’s leave, **shall we**? / Close the door, **will you**?"]},"stage":"5 · Complex sentences","prerequisites":["reported-questions"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
